      "raw_text": "To find the total amount Brenda will have after 3 years, we need to calculate the simple interest earned and add it to the original principal.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the given values.**\n*   Principal ($P$): $300\n*   Time ($t$): 3 years\n*   Interest Rate ($R$): \"three-quarters of the original amount per year\"\n\n**Step 2: Determine the annual interest rate.**\nThe problem states the interest accumulates at a rate of three-quarters of the original amount per year. This means the interest earned in one year is $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the principal.\n$$ \\text{Annual Interest} = P \\times \\frac{3}{4} $$\n$$ \\text{Annual Interest} = 300 \\times 0.75 = 225 $$\nSo, the interest earned per year is $225.\n\n*Note: In standard financial terms, a rate is usually expressed as a percentage (e.g., 7.5%), but the phrasing \"three-quarters of the original amount\" explicitly defines the dollar value of the interest per year directly relative to the principal. We will follow this literal interpretation.*\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the total interest for 3 years.**\nSince the scheme uses simple interest, the interest is calculated only on the original principal and does not compound.\n$$ \\text{Total Interest} = \\text{Annual Interest} \\times t $$\n$$ \\text{Total Interest} = 225 \\times 3 = 675 $$\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the total amount.**\nThe total amount is the sum of the original principal and the total interest earned.\n$$ \\text{Total Amount} = P + \\text{Total Interest} $$\n$$ \\text{Total Amount} = 300 + 675 = 975 $$\n\nAlternatively, using the formula $A = P(1 + rt)$ where $r$ is the rate per year expressed as a fraction:\n$$ r = \\frac{3}{4} $$\n$$ A = 300 \\left(1 + \\frac{3}{4} \\times 3\\right) $$\n$$ A = 300 \\left(1 + \\frac{9}{4}\\right) $$\n$$ A = 300 \\left(\\frac{4}{",
